%% DM26-0670

%% An account is claimed, in two phases, as built (2026-11-04, credentials-agent/common/src/account_claim.rs, docs/architecture/cluster-bootstrap.md). The bootstrap admin is the same flow with a Kubernetes token as the credential, and
%% happens once: the certificate's identity is the account id, which the claimant cannot know before it builds its CSR. Its key is written before the certificate is asked for, so a crash cannot orphan what is issued.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Admin as The claimant
    participant Tool as polar-admin-grant (bootstrap or claim)
    participant Obs as Credentials observer (low trust)
    participant Bk as Cassini broker
    participant CA as Credentials agent (ledger)
    participant CI as cert-issuer

    alt the first admin (bootstrap): someone has to be first, with nothing yet to vouch for them
        Note over Admin,Tool: A Kubernetes ServiceAccount token: controlling the cluster is what proves it.
    else anyone after (claim)
        Note over Admin,CA: An admin's signed action provisioned the account first (ProvisionAccount), by username and email.
    end

    Admin->>Tool: run it, with where the certificate and key go. Neither may exist yet
    Tool->>Tool: make a key, and write it to disk first
    Tool->>Obs: POST /account Begin(claimant): Bootstrap(token) or Native(username, email)
    Obs->>Bk: relayed, the observer judging nothing
    Bk->>CA: delivered
    alt bootstrap
        CA->>CA: the token is the bootstrap identity's, and the bootstrap has not been completed:<br/>the pending account is made now (AccountProvisioned), the only one with no admin's action behind it
    else claim
        CA->>CA: the claimant matches a pending provisioning
    end
    CA-->>Tool: the account_id, through the broker and observer. Not a secret: it ends up in a public certificate
    Note over Tool,CA: Repeating Begin is safe, and gives the same answer until a certificate is issued. A bootstrap counts as done only<br/>then, so one that fails part-way can simply be repeated.

    Tool->>Tool: a CSR whose SAN is that account_id, signed by the claimant's own key
    Tool->>Obs: POST /account Complete(claimant, csr)
    Obs->>Bk: relayed
    Bk->>CA: delivered
    CA->>CA: proves who it is again
    CA->>CI: /internal/issue: an ordinary client certificate, nothing more privileged than an agent's
    CI-->>CA: signed
    CA->>CA: AccountCertificateIssued, which consumes the pending record: a claim succeeds exactly once
    CA-->>Tool: the certificate, through the broker and observer
    Tool->>Tool: checked against the key, then written. Printed if it cannot be written
    Note over Tool,CA: Lost after that, the bootstrap admin's only recovery is rebuilding the cluster. Its certificate then signs admin actions (general/admin_permission_grant).
